def glossary(bead_kb=None):
    """(term, definition) rows of the 'how the quantities are defined' table."""
    bead = f"{bead_kb:.2f} kb" if bead_kb else "an unknown genomic length"
    return [
     ("Epigenetic state", "Each bead carries one of five colours, numbered −2, −1, 0, +1, +2. Positive = open, active, A-like chromatin (red in the plots); negative = closed, quiet, B-like chromatin (blue); 0 = undecided."),
     ("Average epigenetic state", "The mean colour of all beads at one moment, between −2 and +2. Above 0 the region is on average open (A-like), below 0 closed (B-like). It is the average over all beads."),
     ("Epigenetic energy", "A comfort score of the colours: low when beads share the colour of their neighbours and of the beads their loops connect them to, and when early-replicating beads lean towards A. The simulation moves towards lower energy, with some random noise."),
     ("Compartments A and B", "A = open, active, early-replicating neighbourhoods; B = closed, quiet, late-replicating ones. In the model a bead belongs to A if its average colour is clearly positive and to B if clearly negative."),
     ("Domain", "An unbroken stretch of beads that all belong to A or all to B."),
     ("Simulation bead", f"One unit of the polymer. In this run one bead = {bead} of DNA, so lengths are converted to kb where possible."),
     ("LEF, loop length", "A loop extruding factor (e.g. cohesin) has two legs on beads m < n. Its loop is the DNA between the legs; its length is (n − m) beads, i.e. (n − m) × bead size in kb."),
     ("Bound LEF, bound fraction", "A LEF is bound when both legs sit on the chromatin (0 ≤ m < n); otherwise it floats in solution and extrudes nothing. Bound fraction = bound LEFs / all LEFs in one sample."),
     ("Mean loop length (per LEF)", "Average of (n − m) over all LEFs in a sample. Unbound LEFs count as 0, so it falls when loops get smaller and also when LEFs unbind."),
     ("Loop length of bound LEFs", "The same average over bound LEFs only. This is the typical size of an existing loop, used in the summary and phase comparison."),
     ("Legs on CTCF", "Share of bound LEFs whose left leg m sits on a bead with a left-ward CTCF site (L > 0), and whose right leg n sits on a right-ward site (R > 0)."),
     ("Binding potentials L, R", "From the BEDPE anchors: each anchor adds its strength × (1 − o) to L and strength × o to R, where o ∈ [0, 1] is the orientation (0 = left-ward, 1 = right-ward). Without orientation both get the full strength."),
     ("Loop coverage", "Number of loops that span a bead in a sample (loops over a bead). Dark = many nested loops."),
     ("Fork conflict", "A loop that straddles a replication fork in S phase (one leg replicated, the other not). The replication term of the energy penalises it."),
     ("Epigenetic state, compartment", "Every bead has an epigenetic state s ∈ {−2, −1, 0, 1, 2}. s > 0 is A-like (active), s < 0 is B-like (inactive), s = 0 is neutral. The compartment of a bead in a window of samples is A if its mean epigenetic state is above 0.25, B if below −0.25, N otherwise."),
     ("Switch B → A, A → B", "A bead switches when its compartment in a late window differs from its compartment in an earlier window (start vs end of S: windows of 10 % of S). Via N means a change that goes through the neutral state."),
     ("Replication earliness, timing", "From 0 (replicates last) to 1 (replicates first). Replication time of a bead is the moment when it first becomes replicated (T = 0 at the start of S, 1 at the end)."),
     ("T10 / T50 / T90", "Time (fraction of S) at which 10, 50 and 90 % of the region is replicated."),
     ("Initiation rate I(x,t)", "Chance per unit time that an origin fires at position x at time t, estimated from the replication timing curve. Origins are inferred as the earliest-replicating local spots."),
     ("Burn-in, phases", "Burn-in: first samples, discarded while the chain relaxes (grey). G1 = before replication starts, S = replication, G2/M = after it ends."),
     ("Autocorrelation time T_ρ, ESS", "T_ρ: number of consecutive samples that carry the information of one independent sample. ESS = number of samples / T_ρ, the effective number of independent samples."),
     ("Geweke z, split R̂", "Stationarity checks: z compares the start with the end of the chain (|z| < 2 is fine); R̂ compares two halves (below 1.01 is fine)."),
     ("95 % interval", "Mean ± 1.96 × SD / √ESS, which accounts for the autocorrelation of the samples."),
    ]

CAP.update({
 "anchor_map": ("CTCF anchors: map and orientation", "Top (BEDPE only): input loops as arcs (height = length, thickness = strength), coloured by configuration: convergent, tandem, divergent or uncertain. Middle: every anchor with its strength; the marker shows the CTCF orientation (> forward = L, < reverse = R, diamond = ambiguous, circle = unknown). Bottom: how sure each orientation call is.", "Convergent arcs (forward site at the left end, reverse site at the right end) are the loops a LEF can stay trapped in. Many unknown or ambiguous anchors mean the orientation is weak: consider the motif finder."),
 "anchor_stats": ("CTCF anchor statistics", "Left: anchor strength for each orientation class (box = quartiles, dots = anchors). Middle: histogram of the orientation confidence. Right: number of loops per configuration (BEDPE) or of anchors per orientation class (BED / narrowPeak).", "Strong anchors should be confidently oriented, and convergent loops should dominate in a good input."),
 "rt_overview": ("Replication of the region", "Four aligned views along the genome: (1) replication timing data and simulation, (2) the initiation rate I(x,t), (3) the Replikator trajectory with forks and origins, (4) A/B compartments before and after S.", "Early regions (orange) fire first and sit near origins; late regions (purple) are copied last. If replication shapes compartments, the strips in (4) follow the colours of (1)."),
 "epi_switch_traj": ("Compartments over time and switching", "Compartment of every bead over time (red A, blue B, grey undecided), the dashed replication front, how far each bead shifted over S phase (bars on the right) and the share of beads in A and in B (curves below).", "Stable bands in G1. Colours change behind the dashed front when replication reshapes compartments, and settle in G2/M."),
})
HOW.update({
 "story": "Read it top to bottom. The shaded S-phase band marks when forks are active. Faint lines are every sample, bold lines are moving averages with a one-SD band, grey is burn-in. The number on top of each panel is the phase mean. Loop size = mean length of the loops of bound LEFs; compartments = share of beads with positive (A-like) or negative (B-like) epigenetic state; bound LEFs = LEFs with both legs on the chromatin. If loops or compartments move inside the S band and recover afterwards, replication is the driver.",
 "phase_compare": "Each dot is the mean of the quantity in one phase and the bar is its 95 % interval, SD / √ESS, which accounts for autocorrelation. Loop length is the mean size of the loops of bound LEFs; bound LEFs is the share of LEFs attached to DNA; A-like beads is the share of beads with a positive epigenetic state; folding energy is the loop-extrusion energy term. Non-overlapping bars indicate a difference larger than the sampling noise.",
 "anchor_map": "An anchor is the centre of a CTCF site (or loop end). Orientation is P(reverse) = p: forward means p < 0.5 and feeds the left-leg profile L, reverse feeds the right-leg profile R; the confidence is |2p − 1| (0 = coin flip, 1 = certain) and calls below 0.2 are shown as ambiguous. Loop configuration compares the two ends of a loop: convergent = forward then reverse, divergent = reverse then forward, tandem = same orientation, uncertain = at least one end is ambiguous or unknown. In the model a left-ward site (L) can stop the left leg m of a LEF and a right-ward site (R) the right leg n.",
 "anchor_stats": "Strength is the weight of the loop (BEDPE) or the peak signal. Anchors with unknown orientation split their weight evenly between L and R.",
 "rt_overview": "Panel 1: bars are the replication earliness from your data (high = early), the red line is what the simulation produced; ρ measures how well they agree. Panel 2: the initiation rate, which the Replikator calculates from the timing; bright = origins likely fire there and then. Panel 3: position against time; a spot is coloured once it is replicated, with the colour of its timing, so you see replication spread from origins (▲) outward as forks (blue). The curve on the right is the replicated fraction of the region. Panel 4: for every bead, the mean epigenetic state before S (G1) and after S (G2/M), aligned with the timing strip above; ρ tells whether early beads are more A-like.",
 "model_maps": 'Row 1, LEF contacts: every bound LEF with legs m < n adds a contact to the bead pair (m, n). Averaging over the samples of a phase gives C(a, b), the mean number of loops joining beads a and b (symmetrised and slightly blurred). Colour = ln(1 + α·c), where c is C scaled to the range 0 to 1 and α is a fixed display constant, the same in all three phases. Row 2, checkerboard: S(i, j) = s(i)·s(j), where s(i), between -1 and 1, is the phase-averaged epigenetic state of bead i (positive = A-like, negative = B-like). The map is red when the two beads share a compartment and blue when they do not. Row 3, combined: M(i, j) = log[(P(i, j) + C(i, j)) · exp(κ·S(i, j))], with a polymer baseline P(i, j) = 1 / (1 + |i − j|) and a fixed strength κ. This is a first diagnostic from the simulation state alone, not a fit to Hi-C data.',
 "ctcf_occupancy": 'Occupancy of bead $b$: $O(b) \\propto \\sum_{\\ell:\\, b \\in [a_\\ell, c_\\ell]} w_\\ell$, the summed strengths $w_\\ell$ of all loops $\\ell$ whose anchors $a_\\ell$ and $c_\\ell$ enclose $b$ (nested loops stack), scaled to $[0, 1]$. Loading weight: $w(b) \\propto \\varepsilon + (1 - \\varepsilon)\\, O(b)^{\\gamma}$, with floor $\\varepsilon$ and exponent $\\gamma$ set by PREF_BIND_FLOOR and PREF_BIND_POWER; a (re)binding LEF picks bead $b$ with probability proportional to $w(b)$. Orientation score of a site: $\\frac{R - L}{R + L}$ from its left and right binding strengths $L$ and $R$ (columns 8-9 of the BEDPE file); confidence is its absolute value.',
 "loop_length": "Left: mean loop length per LEF with a 95 % interval over LEFs; unbound LEFs count as 0, so it drops when loops shrink and when LEFs unbind. Right: box plot of the loop lengths of bound LEFs in each phase on a log axis (box = middle 50 %, line = median, dot = mean, whiskers = 5th to 95th percentile). Loops span many scales, which is why a log axis is used.",
})
